Q. how did you handle big volume messages sender and receiver while using http adapter in sap cpi?
A. 1. Use Chunking (Splitting Large Messages)
Split large messages into smaller parts inside the iFlow.

Use Splitter (e.g., General Splitter or JSON/XML Splitter) to break big payloads into manageable chunks.

Process each chunk separately.

Example: If a 10 MB JSON file has 10,000 records, split it into 1,000 records per message.

2. Streaming Mode (Large Attachments Handling)
Enable Streaming Mode in the HTTP Receiver Adapter.

Streaming means CPI does not load the full message into memory, it sends/receives in parts.

➔ Reduces memory usage and prevents Out of Memory (OOM) errors.

3. Timeout and Session Settings
Increase Connection Timeout and Socket Timeout settings.

Big messages take more time to send/receive, so you need longer timeouts.

Example: Set timeout to 300,000 ms (5 minutes) or more depending on size.

4. Message Compression
If supported by the receiver, use compression (like GZIP).

Compress large payloads to send faster and save bandwidth.

➔ Add a Content Modifier to set header: Accept-Encoding: gzip

5. Paging / Batch Processing
If the sender or receiver system supports pagination or batch processing, use it.

Fetch or send data in small pages (e.g., 1000 records at a time).

➔ API with page size parameter: GET /data?page=1&size=1000

6. Increase Tenant Resources (If Needed)
If processing a very high number of big messages daily:

Request more memory or message processing capacity from SAP BTP support team.

➔ Called "Tenant Sizing".

7. Use Asynchronous Flows
If possible, design your iFlow to send a response immediately and process data in background.

Use Data Store and Process Direct adapters for background processing.
